
var CF_FULL={"Barbarian|Rage": "You can call on a primal power named Rage, which fills you with remarkable might and resilience. Entering it takes a Bonus Action, and you can do so only if you aren't wearing Heavy armor. The Rages column of the Barbarian Features table shows how many times you can enter it. Finishing a Short Rest restores one expended use, and finishing a Long Rest restores all of them. The rules below apply for as long as your Rage is active. **Damage Resistance.** You gain Resistance to Bludgeoning, Piercing, and Slashing damage. **Rage Damage.** Whenever an attack you make with Strength, using either a weapon or an Unarmed Strike, deals damage to its target, you add a bonus to that damage. The bonus grows as you gain Barbarian levels, as the Rage Damage column of the Barbarian Features table shows. **Strength Advantage.** Your Strength checks and Strength saving throws have Advantage. **No Concentration or Spells.** You are unable to cast spells or keep up Concentration. **Duration.** A Rage runs until your next turn ends. It stops sooner if you put on Heavy armor or gain the Incapacitated condition. If the Rage is still going when your next turn comes, you can keep it up for one more round by doing any one of these things: making an attack roll against an enemy, forcing an enemy to make a saving throw, or taking a Bonus Action to extend it. Every time you extend it, the Rage runs until the end of your following turn. A single Rage can be kept going for no more than 10 minutes.", "Barbarian|Unarmored Defense": "When you wear no armor at all, your base Armor Class is 10 + your Dexterity modifier + your Constitution modifier. Holding a Shield doesn't stop you from getting this benefit.", "Barbarian|Weapon Mastery": "Thanks to your weapons training, you can apply the mastery properties of two kinds of weapons you choose, each of which must be a Simple or Martial Melee weapon (Greataxes and Handaxes, for instance). Each time you finish a Long Rest, you can run through weapon drills and swap one of those choices for a different kind. As you reach higher Barbarian levels, the number of kinds of weapons whose mastery properties you can use goes up, as the Weapon Mastery column of the Barbarian Features table shows: 3 kinds at Barbarian level 4 and 4 kinds at level 10.", "Barbarian|Danger Sense": "You can feel when something is off, which gives you an edge as you avoid hazards. Your Dexterity saving throws have Advantage as long as you don't have the Incapacitated condition.", "Barbarian|Reckless Attack": "You can drop every thought of defense and attack with greater ferocity. As you make the first attack roll on your turn, you can choose to attack recklessly. If you do, then until your next turn starts, your attack rolls that use Strength have Advantage, and attack rolls made against you have Advantage too.", "Barbarian|Primal Knowledge": "Pick one more skill from the list that Barbarians choose from at level 1, and gain proficiency in it. Also, while your Rage is active, you can draw on primal power for certain tasks: whenever you make an ability check with Acrobatics, Intimidation, Perception, Stealth, or Survival, you can make it as a Strength check, even when that skill normally relies on another ability. Used this way, your Strength stands for the primal power flowing through you and sharpening your agility, bearing, and senses.", "Barbarian|Extra Attack": "On your turn, taking the Attack action lets you attack two times rather than once.", "Barbarian|Fast Movement": "As long as you aren't wearing Heavy armor, your Speed is 10 feet higher.", "Barbarian|Feral Instinct": "Your instincts are sharp enough that your Initiative rolls have Advantage.", "Barbarian|Instinctive Pounce": "When you take the Bonus Action to enter your Rage, you can also move up to half your Speed as part of that same Bonus Action.", "Barbarian|Brutal Strike": "When you use Reckless Attack, you can give up any Advantage on one attack roll of your choice that uses Strength on your turn, as long as that roll doesn't have Disadvantage. If that attack hits, it deals an extra 1d10 damage to the target, using the same damage type as the weapon or Unarmed Strike, and you can apply one Brutal Strike effect you choose from the options below. **Forceful Blow.** You push the target 15 feet straight away from you. Then you can move up to half your Speed in a straight line toward it, and this movement doesn't provoke Opportunity Attacks. **Hamstring Blow.** Until the start of your next turn, the target's Speed is 15 feet lower. A target can be under only one Hamstring Blow at a time, and the most recent one is the one that counts.", "Barbarian|Relentless Rage": "Your Rage lets you keep fighting through terrible wounds. If your Rage is active when you drop to 0 Hit Points and you aren't killed outright, you can attempt a Constitution saving throw with a DC of 10. On a success, your Hit Points become a number equal to twice your Barbarian level instead. Every use of this feature after the first raises the DC by 5. The DC goes back to 10 once you finish a Short or Long Rest.", "Barbarian|Improved Brutal Strike": "You have learned new ways to strike with fury. At Barbarian level 13, the two effects below join your Brutal Strike options. At level 17, the extra damage from Brutal Strike becomes 2d10, and each time you use Brutal Strike you can apply two different Brutal Strike effects. **Staggering Blow.** The target has Disadvantage on its next saving throw, and until your next turn starts, it can't make Opportunity Attacks. **Sundering Blow.** The next attack roll another creature makes against the target before your next turn starts gains a +5 bonus. A single attack roll can't benefit from more than one Sundering Blow bonus.", "Barbarian|Persistent Rage": "When you roll Initiative, you can recover all expended uses of Rage. Once you recover uses this way, you can't do it again until you finish a Long Rest. Also, your Rage is now fierce enough to last 10 minutes with no need for you to extend it each round. It ends early if you have the Unconscious condition (having only the Incapacitated condition isn't enough) or if you put on Heavy armor.", "Barbarian|Indomitable Might": "When the total of a Strength check or Strength saving throw you make is lower than your Strength score, you can use the score as the total instead.", "Barbarian|Epic Boon": "Choose an Epic Boon feat or another feat you qualify for, and gain it. Boon of Irresistible Offense is the suggested pick.", "Barbarian|Primal Champion": "You are the embodiment of primal power. Your Strength score and your Constitution score each increase by 4, up to a maximum of 25.", "Bard|Bardic Inspiration": "Through words, music, or dance, you can inspire others with supernatural power. Your Bardic Inspiration die stands for this inspiration, and it starts as a d6. **Using Bardic Inspiration.** You can take a Bonus Action to inspire another creature that is within 60 feet of you and can see or hear you. That creature receives one of your Bardic Inspiration dice. A creature can never hold more than one Bardic Inspiration die simultaneously. If the creature fails a D20 Test within the next hour, it can roll the die and add the number to the d20, possibly changing the failure into a success. It can do this only once, because the die is used up as soon as it's rolled. **Number of Uses.** The number of times you can give out a Bardic Inspiration die equals your Charisma modifier, with a minimum of one. Finishing a Long Rest restores all expended uses. **At Higher Levels.** At certain Bard levels the die gets bigger, as the Bardic Die column of the Bard Features table shows: a d8 at level 5, a d10 at level 10, and a d12 at level 15.", "Bard|Spellcasting": "Your bardic arts let you cast spells. You follow the general spellcasting rules, applied to Bard spells as explained here. Bard spells are found in the Bard spell list. **Cantrips.** Choose two cantrips from the Bard spell list to know. Dancing Lights and Vicious Mockery are suggested. Each time you gain a Bard level, you can swap one of your cantrips for a different cantrip from the Bard spell list. At Bard levels 4 and 10, you learn one more cantrip of your choice from that list, as the Cantrips column of the Bard Features table shows. **Spell Slots.** The Bard Features table lists the spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** With this feature, you can cast the level 1+ spells on a list you prepare. To begin, your list holds four level 1 spells that you pick from the Bard spell list. The suggested picks are Charm Person, Color Spray, Dissonant Whispers, and Healing Word. Your list grows as you gain Bard levels, following the Prepared Spells column of the Bard Features table. Each time that number goes up, pick more spells from the Bard spell list until your list is as long as the table says. Each chosen spell has to be of a level you have spell slots for. As an example, a level 3 Bard can have six spells of levels 1 and 2, in any mix, on the prepared list. Spells that another Bard feature makes always prepared don't count toward the number you can prepare with this feature, but they count as Bard spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** Each time you gain a Bard level, you can swap one spell on your list for a different Bard spell of a level you have spell slots for. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your Bard spells use Charisma as their spellcasting ability. **Spellcasting Focus.** A Musical Instrument can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Bard spells.", "Bard|Expertise": "Pick two of your skill proficiencies and gain Expertise in them, which doubles your Proficiency Bonus for ability checks that use those skills. If you have proficiency in Performance and Persuasion, those two are suggested. When you reach Bard level 9, pick two more of your skill proficiencies to gain Expertise in.", "Bard|Jack of All Trades": "When you make an ability check that uses a skill proficiency you don't have and that doesn't already use your Proficiency Bonus, you can add half your Proficiency Bonus to it, rounded down. For instance, if you make a Strength (Athletics) check without proficiency in Athletics, half your Proficiency Bonus can be added to that check.", "Bard|Font of Inspiration": "Finishing a Short Rest or a Long Rest now restores every expended use of your Bardic Inspiration. You can also expend a spell slot, without taking an action, to recover one expended use of it.", "Bard|Countercharm": "Using musical notes or words of power, you can disrupt effects that sway the mind. If a creature within 30 feet of you, or you yourself, fails a saving throw against an effect that inflicts the Charmed or Frightened condition, your Reaction can be used to force a reroll of that save, and the new roll is made with Advantage.", "Bard|Magical Secrets": "Secrets from many magical traditions are now yours. At this Bard level and every later one where the Prepared Spells number in the Bard Features table rises, any of your new prepared spells can come from the Bard, Cleric, Druid, or Wizard spell list. Those chosen spells count as Bard spells for you. In addition, when you replace a spell you prepared for this class, you can pick the new spell from any of those lists.", "Bard|Superior Inspiration": "When you roll Initiative and have fewer than two uses of Bardic Inspiration left, you regain expended uses until you have two.", "Bard|Epic Boon": "Choose an Epic Boon feat or another feat you qualify for, and gain it. Boon of Spell Recall is the suggested pick.", "Bard|Words of Creation": "Of the Words of Creation, you have mastered two: the words of life and of death. Power Word Heal and Power Word Kill are therefore always among your prepared spells. Whenever you cast one of them, you can have it target a second creature too, as long as that creature is no more than 10 feet from the first target.", "Cleric|Spellcasting": "Through prayer and meditation, you have learned to cast spells. You follow the general spellcasting rules, applied to Cleric spells as explained here. Cleric spells are found in the Cleric spell list. **Cantrips.** Choose three cantrips from the Cleric spell list to know. Guidance, Sacred Flame, and Thaumaturgy are suggested. Each time you gain a Cleric level, you can swap one of your cantrips for a different cantrip from the Cleric spell list. At Cleric levels 4 and 10, you learn one more cantrip of your choice from that list, as the Cantrips column of the Cleric Features table shows. **Spell Slots.** The Cleric Features table lists the spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** With this feature, you can cast the level 1+ spells on a list you prepare. To begin, your list holds four level 1 Cleric spells of your choice. The suggested ones are Bless, Cure Wounds, Guiding Bolt, and Shield of Faith. Your list grows as you gain Cleric levels, following the Prepared Spells column of the Cleric Features table. Each time that number goes up, pick more spells from the Cleric spell list until your list is as long as the table says. Each chosen spell has to be of a level you have spell slots for. As an example, a level 3 Cleric can have six spells of levels 1 and 2, in any mix, on the prepared list. Spells that another Cleric feature makes always prepared don't count toward the number you can prepare with this feature, but they count as Cleric spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** Each time you finish a Long Rest, you can change your prepared list, swapping any of its spells for other Cleric spells of levels you have spell slots for. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your Cleric spells use Wisdom as their spellcasting ability. **Spellcasting Focus.** A Holy Symbol can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Cleric spells.", "Cleric|Divine Order": "You have devoted yourself to one of these sacred roles, chosen by you. **Protector.** Trained for battle, you are proficient with Martial weapons and have Heavy armor training. **Thaumaturge.** Choose one extra cantrip to know from the Cleric spell list. Also, thanks to your mystical bond with the divine, your Intelligence (Arcana) and Intelligence (Religion) checks get a bonus equal to your Wisdom modifier, which is at least +1.", "Cleric|Channel Divinity": "Divine energy from the Outer Planes flows through you, and you can direct it to fuel magical effects. Two such effects are available to you from the start, Divine Spark and Turn Undead, and both are explained below. Whenever you use this class's Channel Divinity, choose which of its effects you create. You unlock more effect options at higher Cleric levels. You have two uses of this class's Channel Divinity. A Short Rest gives back one expended use when you finish it, and a Long Rest gives back all of them. Some Cleric levels grant extra uses, shown in the Cleric Features table under Channel Divinity: 3 uses at level 6 and 4 at level 18. If one of these effects forces a saving throw, use the spell save DC of this class's Spellcasting feature. **Divine Spark.** You take a Magic action to point your Holy Symbol toward another creature within 30 feet of you that you can see, and you focus divine energy on that creature. Roll 1d8 and add your Wisdom modifier to the roll. With that total, you either restore that many Hit Points to the creature or force it to make a Constitution saving throw. On a failure, it takes Necrotic or Radiant damage (you choose which) equal to the total. On a success, it takes half as much, rounded down. The number of d8s goes up by one at Cleric levels 7 (2d8), 13 (3d8), and 18 (4d8). **Turn Undead.** You take a Magic action to raise your Holy Symbol and rebuke Undead creatures. Every Undead you choose within 30 feet of you must attempt a Wisdom saving throw. Any creature that fails gains the Frightened and Incapacitated conditions for 1 minute, and for that time it tries on each of its turns to get as far away from you as possible. For a given creature, the effect ends early when it takes any damage. It also ends early if you gain the Incapacitated condition or die.", "Cleric|Sear Undead": "Each time you use Turn Undead, you can roll d8s equal in number to your Wisdom modifier (at least one d8) and add them up. Any Undead that fails its saving throw against that particular use takes the sum as Radiant damage. The Turn Undead effect doesn't end because of this damage.", "Cleric|Blessed Strikes": "Divine power fills you during combat. Pick one of the options below to gain. If a Cleric subclass from an older book also grants either option, you use only the option you pick here. **Divine Strike.** If an attack roll you make with a weapon hits a creature, you can deal an extra 1d8 damage to the target, and you choose whether that damage is Necrotic or Radiant. You can do this only once on each of your turns. **Potent Spellcasting.** When you deal damage with a Cleric cantrip, add your Wisdom modifier to that damage.", "Cleric|Divine Intervention": "You can ask your deity or pantheon to step in for you. As a Magic action, pick a Cleric spell no higher than level 5 that isn't cast with a Reaction. As part of that same action, you cast it without expending a spell slot and without needing Material components. After you use this feature, you must finish a Long Rest before you can use it again.", "Cleric|Improved Blessed Strikes": "Whichever Blessed Strikes option you picked becomes stronger. **Divine Strike.** Your Divine Strike's extra damage becomes 2d8. **Potent Spellcasting.** When you cast a Cleric cantrip and it deals damage to a creature, you can grant vitality to yourself or to another creature within 60 feet of you, giving Temporary Hit Points equal to twice your Wisdom modifier.", "Cleric|Epic Boon": "Choose an Epic Boon feat or another feat you qualify for, and gain it. Boon of Fate is the suggested pick.", "Cleric|Greater Divine Intervention": "You can now call on even mightier divine help. When you use Divine Intervention and pick a spell, you can pick Wish. If you do, Divine Intervention becomes unavailable to you until you have finished 2d4 Long Rests.", "Druid|Spellcasting": "By studying the mystical forces of nature, you have learned to cast spells. You follow the general spellcasting rules, applied to Druid spells as explained here. Druid spells are found in the Druid spell list. **Cantrips.** Choose two cantrips from the Druid spell list to know. Druidcraft and Produce Flame are suggested. Each time you gain a Druid level, you can swap one of your cantrips for a different cantrip from the Druid spell list. At Druid levels 4 and 10, you learn one more cantrip of your choice from that list, as the Cantrips column of the Druid Features table shows. **Spell Slots.** The Druid Features table lists the spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** With this feature, you can cast the level 1+ spells on a list you prepare. To begin, your list holds four level 1 Druid spells of your choice. The suggested ones are Animal Friendship, Cure Wounds, Faerie Fire, and Thunderwave. Your list grows as you gain Druid levels, following the Prepared Spells column of the Druid Features table. Each time that number goes up, pick more spells from the Druid spell list until your list is as long as the table says. Each chosen spell has to be of a level you have spell slots for. As an example, a level 3 Druid can have six spells of levels 1 and 2, in any mix, on the prepared list. Spells that another Druid feature makes always prepared don't count toward the number you can prepare with this feature, but they count as Druid spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** Each time you finish a Long Rest, you can change your prepared list, swapping any of its spells for other Druid spells of levels you have spell slots for. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your Druid spells use Wisdom as their spellcasting ability. **Spellcasting Focus.** A Druidic Focus can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Druid spells.", "Druid|Druidic": "You know Druidic, the secret language of Druids. Learning this ancient tongue also opened the magic of speaking with animals to you, so the Speak with Animals spell is always prepared for you. You can leave hidden messages written in Druidic. You and anyone else who knows Druidic notice such a message automatically. Other creatures notice that the message is there by succeeding on a DC 15 Intelligence (Investigation) check, but they can't decipher it without magic.", "Druid|Primal Order": "You have devoted yourself to one of these sacred roles, chosen by you. **Magician.** Choose one extra cantrip to know from the Druid spell list. Also, thanks to your mystical bond with nature, your Intelligence (Arcana) and Intelligence (Nature) checks get a bonus equal to your Wisdom modifier, which is at least +1. **Warden.** Trained for battle, you are proficient with Martial weapons and have Medium armor training.", "Druid|Wild Shape": "Nature's power lets you take on an animal's form. As a Bonus Action, you change shape into one of the Beast forms that this feature has taught you (see Known Forms below). The form lasts for a duration in hours that equals half your Druid level. It ends sooner if you use Wild Shape again, gain the Incapacitated condition, or die. Taking a Bonus Action also lets you leave the form before then. **Number of Uses.** Wild Shape can be used twice. Finishing a Short Rest restores one expended use, and finishing a Long Rest restores all of them. Certain Druid levels give you more uses, as the Wild Shape column of the Druid Features table shows: 3 at level 6 and 4 at level 17. **Known Forms.** Four Beast forms are known to you for this feature, picked from Beast stat blocks with a Challenge Rating of 1/4 or lower and no Fly Speed (appendix B has stat blocks to choose from). Rat, Riding Horse, Spider, and Wolf are suggested. Each time you finish a Long Rest, you can swap one known form for another form that qualifies. At certain Druid levels, the number of forms you know and their maximum Challenge Rating go up, as the Beast Shapes table shows: 6 forms of Challenge Rating 1/2 or lower at level 4, and 8 forms of Challenge Rating 1 or lower at level 8. Starting at level 8, you can also take a form that has a Fly Speed. If the Dungeon Master allows it, you can search the Monster Manual or other sources for qualifying Beasts when you choose known forms. **Rules While Shape-Shifted.** In a Wild Shape form, you keep your personality, your memories, and your ability to speak, and these rules apply. **Temporary Hit Points.** Every time you take on a Wild Shape form, your Druid level sets how many Temporary Hit Points you gain. **Game Statistics.** The Beast's stat block replaces your game statistics, except that you keep your feats, languages, class features, Hit Points, Hit Point Dice, creature type, and your Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma scores. You also keep your skill and saving throw proficiencies, using your Proficiency Bonus for them, and you gain the creature's proficiencies as well. When the Beast's stat block shows a higher skill or saving throw modifier than yours, use the stat block's modifier. **No Spellcasting.** You can't cast spells. Changing shape doesn't break your Concentration, though, and doesn't otherwise disrupt a spell you have already cast. **Objects.** The form's limbs, not your own, decide how well you can handle objects. You also choose whether your equipment falls to the ground in your space, merges into the new form, or is worn by it. Worn equipment works normally, but the Dungeon Master decides whether the new form can practically wear a given item, depending on the creature's shape and size. Your gear doesn't resize or reshape itself to fit the new form, and anything the form can't wear has to either drop to the ground or merge with it. Merged equipment has no effect while you remain in that form.", "Druid|Wild Companion": "You can call a nature spirit that takes an animal's shape to help you. As a Magic action, you can cast Find Familiar without Material components by expending either a spell slot or one use of Wild Shape. A familiar summoned this way is Fey, and it vanishes when you finish a Long Rest.", "Druid|Wild Resurgence": "If you have no Wild Shape uses remaining, you can expend a spell slot to gain one use, once on each of your turns. This takes no action. You can also expend one Wild Shape use, again taking no action, to gain a level 1 spell slot. After you do so, you can't do it again until you finish a Long Rest.", "Druid|Elemental Fury": "Elemental might flows through you. Pick one of the options below to gain. **Potent Spellcasting.** When you deal damage with a Druid cantrip, add your Wisdom modifier to that damage. **Primal Strike.** If an attack roll you make with a weapon, or with a Beast form's attack while in Wild Shape, hits a creature, you can deal an extra 1d8 damage to the target. Its type is Cold, Fire, Lightning, or Thunder, chosen by you when you hit. You can do this only once on each of your turns.", "Druid|Improved Elemental Fury": "Whichever Elemental Fury option you picked becomes stronger, as described here. **Potent Spellcasting.** When you cast a Druid cantrip whose range is 10 feet or more, its range grows by 300 feet. **Primal Strike.** Your Primal Strike's extra damage becomes 2d8.", "Druid|Beast Spells": "While in a Wild Shape form, you can cast spells as a Beast. The exception is any spell whose Material component has a listed cost or is consumed by the spell.", "Druid|Epic Boon": "Choose an Epic Boon feat or another feat you qualify for, and gain it. Boon of Dimensional Travel is the suggested pick.", "Druid|Archdruid": "Nature's vitality is always blooming inside you, which gives you the benefits below. **Evergreen Wild Shape.** When you roll Initiative while you have no Wild Shape uses left, you regain one expended use of it. **Nature Magician.** You can turn uses of Wild Shape into a spell slot, which takes no action. Pick some of your unexpended Wild Shape uses and convert them into one spell slot, where each use counts for 2 spell levels. For example, converting two uses of Wild Shape creates a level 4 spell slot. Once you have used this benefit, you must finish a Long Rest before using it again. **Longevity.** The primal magic you command slows your aging. Your body ages just one year for every ten years that go by.", "Fighter|Fighting Style": "You have sharpened your martial skill and gain a Fighting Style feat of your choice. Defense is the suggested pick. Each time you gain a Fighter level, you can swap that feat for a different Fighting Style feat.", "Fighter|Second Wind": "You can draw on a limited reserve of physical and mental stamina. As a Bonus Action, you can tap into it to heal yourself: roll 1d10, add your Fighter level, and regain that many Hit Points. This feature has two uses. Finishing a Short Rest restores one expended use, and finishing a Long Rest restores all of them. Certain Fighter levels give you more uses, as the Second Wind column of the Fighter Features table shows: 3 at level 4 and 4 at level 10.", "Fighter|Weapon Mastery": "Thanks to your weapons training, you can apply the mastery properties of three kinds of weapons you choose, each of which must be a Simple or Martial weapon. Each time you finish a Long Rest, you can run through weapon drills and swap one of those choices for a different kind. As you reach higher Fighter levels, the number of kinds of weapons whose mastery properties you can use goes up, as the Weapon Mastery column of the Fighter Features table shows: 4 kinds at Fighter level 4, 5 at level 10, and 6 at level 16.", "Fighter|Action Surge": "For a moment you can push past your usual limits. On your turn, you can take one extra action, as long as it isn't the Magic action. After you use this feature, you must finish a Short or Long Rest before you can use it again. From level 17, you can use it twice between rests, but no more than once on the same turn.", "Fighter|Tactical Mind": "You think tactically, whether or not you are in battle. If an ability check you make fails, you can expend one use of your Second Wind to push toward success. You don't regain Hit Points. Instead, roll 1d10 and add the number to the check, which might make it succeed. Should the check fail anyway, you keep that use of Second Wind.", "Fighter|Extra Attack": "On your turn, taking the Attack action lets you attack two times rather than once.", "Fighter|Tactical Shift": "Each time your Bonus Action activates Second Wind, you can also move a distance of up to half your Speed. Opportunity Attacks aren't provoked by this movement.", "Fighter|Indomitable": "If you fail a saving throw, you can roll it again with a bonus to the roll equal to your Fighter level. The new roll is the one you must use. After that, the feature is unavailable until you finish a Long Rest. At level 13 you can use it two times between Long Rests, and at level 17, three times.", "Fighter|Tactical Master": "If a weapon's mastery property is one you can use, then whenever you attack with that weapon, you can trade the property for Push, Sap, or Slow on that one attack.", "Fighter|Two Extra Attacks": "On your turn, taking the Attack action lets you attack three times rather than once.", "Fighter|Studied Attacks": "You study your foes and learn from every attack you make. When you miss a creature with an attack roll, your next attack roll against it has Advantage if you make it before your next turn ends.", "Fighter|Epic Boon": "Choose an Epic Boon feat or another feat you qualify for, and gain it. Boon of Combat Prowess is the suggested pick.", "Fighter|Three Extra Attacks": "On your turn, taking the Attack action lets you attack four times rather than once.", "Monk|Martial Arts": "Your martial arts practice gives you command of fighting styles built around your Unarmed Strike and Monk weapons. Monk weapons are Simple Melee weapons and Martial Melee weapons with the Light property. You gain the benefits below while you are unarmed or wielding only Monk weapons, provided you wear no armor and wield no Shield. **Bonus Unarmed Strike.** As a Bonus Action, you can make an Unarmed Strike. **Martial Arts Die.** You can roll 1d6 for the damage of your Unarmed Strike or Monk weapons instead of their usual damage. As your Monk level rises, the die changes, following the Martial Arts column of the Monk Features table: from level 5 it is 1d8, from level 11 it is 1d10, and from level 17 it is 1d12. **Dexterous Attacks.** Your Unarmed Strikes and Monk weapons can use your Dexterity modifier rather than your Strength modifier for their attack and damage rolls. Also, when your Unarmed Strike uses its Grapple or Shove option, the save DC can be based on your Dexterity modifier rather than your Strength modifier.", "Monk|Unarmored Defense": "Your base Armor Class equals 10 + your Dexterity modifier + your Wisdom modifier while you wear no armor and wield no Shield.", "Monk|Monk's Focus": "Your focus and martial training let you tap a well of extraordinary energy within you, represented by Focus Points. How many points you have depends on your Monk level, as shown in the Monk Features table under Focus Points (the number equals your Monk level). These points can be spent to fuel or enhance certain Monk features. At first you know three such features, each described below (Flurry of Blows, Patient Defense, Step of the Wind). An expended Focus Point stays spent until you finish a Short or Long Rest, and when that rest ends you regain every expended point. If a feature that uses Focus Points calls for a target's saving throw, the DC is 8 + your Wisdom modifier + your Proficiency Bonus. **Flurry of Blows.** You can make two Unarmed Strikes as a Bonus Action by spending 1 Focus Point. **Patient Defense.** Your Bonus Action can be used to take the Disengage action. You can instead spend 1 Focus Point to take the Disengage action and the Dodge action together with one Bonus Action. **Step of the Wind.** Your Bonus Action can be used to take the Dash action. You can instead spend 1 Focus Point to take the Disengage action and the Dash action together with one Bonus Action, and your jump distance doubles for the turn.", "Monk|Unarmored Movement": "Your Speed rises by 10 feet while you wear no armor and wield no Shield. At certain Monk levels this bonus gets larger, as the Unarmored Movement column of the Monk Features table shows: +15 feet at level 6, +20 feet at level 10, +25 feet at level 14, and +30 feet at level 18.", "Monk|Uncanny Metabolism": "As you roll Initiative, you can choose to regain every expended Focus Point. When you do, roll your Martial Arts die, and you regain Hit Points equal to the roll plus your Monk level. Once you have used this feature, it is unavailable until you finish a Long Rest.", "Monk|Deflect Attacks": "When an attack roll hits you and the damage includes any Bludgeoning, Piercing, or Slashing damage, you can use your Reaction to cut the total damage that attack deals to you. You reduce it by 1d10 + your Dexterity modifier + your Monk level. If the damage drops to 0 this way, you can expend 1 Focus Point to redirect part of the attack's force. If the attack was a melee attack, choose a creature within 5 feet of you that you can see. If it was a ranged attack, choose a creature within 60 feet of you that you can see and that isn't behind Total Cover. The chosen creature makes a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, it takes damage equal to two rolls of your Martial Arts die + your Dexterity modifier, of the same type the attack dealt.", "Monk|Slow Fall": "When you fall, you can take a Reaction to lower any damage that the fall deals to you. The reduction equals five times your Monk level.", "Monk|Extra Attack": "On your turn, taking the Attack action lets you attack two times rather than once.", "Monk|Stunning Strike": "If your Monk weapon or Unarmed Strike hits a creature, you can expend 1 Focus Point to try for a stunning strike, but no more than once per turn. The target must attempt a Constitution saving throw. On a failure, the target gains the Stunned condition, which lasts until your next turn begins. On a success, the target's Speed is halved until your next turn begins, and the next attack roll against the target before that point has Advantage.", "Monk|Empowered Strikes": "When your Unarmed Strike deals damage, you can have it deal Force damage or its normal damage type, whichever you choose.", "Monk|Evasion": "Some effects let you make a Dexterity saving throw so that you take only half damage. Against such an effect, a successful save means you take no damage, and a failed save means you take only half damage. You lose this benefit while you have the Incapacitated condition.", "Monk|Acrobatic Movement": "While you wear no armor and wield no Shield, you can move across liquids and along vertical surfaces during your turn without falling while you move.", "Monk|Heightened Focus": "**Flurry of Blows.** When you spend 1 Focus Point to use Flurry of Blows, it lets you make three Unarmed Strikes rather than two. **Patient Defense.** Whenever you spend a Focus Point to use Patient Defense, you also gain Temporary Hit Points. Roll your Martial Arts die twice, and the total is the number you gain. **Step of the Wind.** If you spend a Focus Point on Step of the Wind, you can pick a willing creature that is Large or smaller and within 5 feet of you. That creature travels along with you until your turn ends, and its movement doesn't provoke Opportunity Attacks.", "Monk|Self-Restoration": "At the end of each of your turns, your sheer force of will lets you rid yourself of one of these conditions: Charmed, Frightened, or Poisoned. You also don't gain Exhaustion levels from going without food and drink.", "Monk|Deflect Energy": "Your Deflect Attacks feature now works against attacks of any damage type, not only those that deal Bludgeoning, Piercing, or Slashing damage.", "Monk|Disciplined Survivor": "Your discipline of body and mind makes you proficient in every saving throw. In addition, whenever you fail a saving throw, you can spend 1 Focus Point to roll the save again. You must use the new roll.", "Monk|Perfect Focus": "If you hold 3 or fewer Focus Points when you roll Initiative without using Uncanny Metabolism, your expended Focus Points are restored until you have 4.", "Monk|Superior Defense": "At the start of your turn, you can spend 3 Focus Points to brace against harm. Until 1 minute passes or you have the Incapacitated condition, you have Resistance to every type of damage except Force damage.", "Monk|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. The recommended choice is Boon of Irresistible Offense.", "Monk|Body and Mind": "Your body and mind have reached new heights of development. Your Dexterity and Wisdom scores each increase by 4, to a maximum of 25.", "Paladin|Lay on Hands": "Your blessed touch heals wounds. You have a pool of healing power that refills when you finish a Long Rest. The pool can restore up to five times your Paladin level in Hit Points in total. As a Bonus Action, you can draw on the pool to restore Hit Points to a creature you touch, which can be yourself. You can restore any amount up to what the pool still holds. You can also remove the Poisoned condition from the creature by spending 5 Hit Points from the pool. Points spent this way don't also restore Hit Points to the creature.", "Paladin|Spellcasting": "Through prayer and meditation, you have learned to cast spells. The rules below show how you cast your Paladin spells, which come from the Paladin spell list. **Spell Slots.** The Paladin Features table lists how many spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all your expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** You prepare a list of the level 1+ spells you can cast with this feature. To begin, pick two level 1 Paladin spells. Recommended picks are Heroism and Searing Smite. The Prepared Spells column of the Paladin Features table shows how your list grows as you gain Paladin levels. Each time that number goes up, pick more Paladin spells until your list holds as many spells as the table shows. Each chosen spell's level must be one for which you have spell slots. For instance, a level 5 Paladin's prepared list can hold six spells from the Paladin list, with any mix of levels 1 and 2. If another Paladin feature gives you spells that are always prepared, those spells don't count against the number you can prepare with this feature, but they still count as Paladin spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** After you finish a Long Rest, you can swap one spell on your list for a different Paladin spell, as long as you have spell slots of that spell's level. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your spellcasting ability for Paladin spells is Charisma. **Spellcasting Focus.** A Holy Symbol can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Paladin spells.", "Paladin|Weapon Mastery": "Choose two kinds of weapons you're proficient with, such as Longswords and Javelins. Your weapon training lets you use the mastery properties of those weapons. After you finish a Long Rest, you can change which kinds of weapons you chose. For instance, you could trade them for Halberds and Flails.", "Paladin|Fighting Style": "You gain a Fighting Style feat of your choice. You can take the option below in place of one of those feats. **Blessed Warrior.** Pick two Cleric cantrips to learn. Guidance and Sacred Flame are recommended. For you, these cantrips count as Paladin spells, and you use Charisma as your spellcasting ability for them. Each time you gain a Paladin level, you can swap one of these cantrips for a different Cleric cantrip.", "Paladin|Paladin's Smite": "After you hit a creature with a melee weapon or an Unarmed Strike, you can cast Divine Smite right away. Casting it takes a Bonus Action. Divine Smite is always prepared for you. It deals 2d8 Radiant damage, plus 1d8 against a Fiend or an Undead, plus 1d8 for each spell slot level above 1. You can also cast it without expending a spell slot. After you cast it this way, you need to finish a Long Rest before casting it without a slot again.", "Paladin|Channel Divinity": "You can draw divine energy straight from the Outer Planes and use it to power magical effects. Your first such effect is Divine Sense, detailed below. Other Paladin features give you more Channel Divinity effect options. Whenever you use it, you choose which of this class's effects to create. This class's Channel Divinity has two uses. When you finish a Short Rest, you regain one expended use, and when you finish a Long Rest, you regain all of them. At Paladin level 11, you gain one more use. When a Channel Divinity effect calls for a saving throw, use the spell save DC of this class's Spellcasting feature. **Divine Sense.** As a Bonus Action, you can attune your senses to Celestials, Fiends, and Undead. Until 10 minutes pass or you have the Incapacitated condition, you know where any creature of those types is if it's within 60 feet of you, and you know its creature type. In that same radius, you also sense the presence of any consecrated or desecrated place or object, as the Hallow spell does.", "Paladin|Extra Attack": "Each time you take the Attack action during your turn, you can make two attacks rather than one.", "Paladin|Faithful Steed": "You can call on an otherworldly steed for aid. The Find Steed spell is always prepared for you. You can also cast it once without expending a spell slot, and finishing a Long Rest restores that ability.", "Paladin|Aura of Protection": "You give off a protective aura that can't be seen. It fills a 10-foot Emanation originating from you. While you have the Incapacitated condition, the aura doesn't function. While in the aura, you and your allies add a bonus equal to your Charisma modifier to your saving throws (the bonus is at least +1). When more than one Paladin is present, a creature gains the benefit of only one Aura of Protection at a time, choosing which one applies while it is inside several.", "Paladin|Abjure Foes": "As a Magic action, you can spend one use of this class's Channel Divinity to fill your foes with awe. Presenting your Holy Symbol or weapon, you can choose creatures you can see within 60 feet of you as targets. The number of targets equals your Charisma modifier, with a minimum of one. Every target must make a Wisdom saving throw. If it fails, it has the Frightened condition until it takes any damage or 1 minute passes. While Frightened this way, a target can, on each of its turns, either move, take an action, or take a Bonus Action, but only one of those.", "Paladin|Aura of Courage": "You and your allies gain Immunity to the Frightened condition while inside your Aura of Protection. When an ally who has the Frightened condition enters the aura, the condition doesn't affect that ally as long as it stays there.", "Paladin|Radiant Strikes": "Supernatural power now fills your strikes. Whenever an attack roll you make with a Melee weapon or an Unarmed Strike hits a target, that target takes an extra 1d8 Radiant damage.", "Paladin|Restoring Touch": "Using Lay On Hands on a creature also lets you end one or more of these conditions on it: Blinded, Charmed, Deafened, Frightened, Paralyzed, or Stunned. Each condition you end costs 5 Hit Points from your Lay On Hands healing pool, and the creature regains no Hit Points from points spent this way.", "Paladin|Aura Expansion": "Your Aura of Protection now fills a 30-foot Emanation.", "Paladin|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. The recommended choice is Boon of Truesight.", "Ranger|Spellcasting": "You have learned to channel nature's magical essence to cast spells. The rules below show how you cast your Ranger spells, which come from the Ranger spell list. **Spell Slots.** The Ranger Features table lists how many spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all your expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** You prepare a list of the level 1+ spells you can cast with this feature. To begin, pick two level 1 Ranger spells. Recommended picks are Cure Wounds and Ensnaring Strike. The Prepared Spells column of the Ranger Features table shows how your list grows as you gain Ranger levels. Each time that number goes up, pick more Ranger spells until your list holds as many spells as the table shows. Each chosen spell's level must be one for which you have spell slots. For instance, a level 5 Ranger's prepared list can hold six spells from the Ranger list, with any mix of levels 1 and 2. If another Ranger feature gives you spells that are always prepared, those spells don't count against the number you can prepare with this feature, but they still count as Ranger spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** After you finish a Long Rest, you can swap one spell on your list for a different Ranger spell, as long as you have spell slots of that spell's level. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your spellcasting ability for Ranger spells is Wisdom. **Spellcasting Focus.** A Druidic Focus can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Ranger spells.", "Ranger|Favored Enemy": "Hunter's Mark is always prepared for you. You can cast it two times without expending a spell slot, and finishing a Long Rest restores all expended uses of this ability. At certain Ranger levels, you gain more of these slot-free castings, as the Favored Enemy column of the Ranger Features table shows: 2 at level 1, 3 at level 5, 4 at level 9, 5 at level 13, and 6 at level 17.", "Ranger|Weapon Mastery": "Choose two kinds of weapons you're proficient with, such as Longbows and Shortswords. Your weapon training lets you use the mastery properties of those weapons. After you finish a Long Rest, you can change which kinds of weapons you chose. For instance, you could trade them for Scimitars and Longswords.", "Ranger|Deft Explorer": "**Expertise.** Choose one skill you're proficient in but don't yet have Expertise in. You gain Expertise in that skill. **Languages.** You learn two languages of your choice, picked from the Standard Languages and Rare Languages tables.", "Ranger|Fighting Style": "You gain a Fighting Style feat of your choice. You can take the option below in place of one of those feats. **Druidic Warrior.** Pick two Druid cantrips to learn. Recommended picks are Guidance and Starry Wisp. For you, these cantrips count as Ranger spells, and you use Wisdom as your spellcasting ability for them. Each time you gain a Ranger level, you can swap one of these cantrips for a different Druid cantrip.", "Ranger|Extra Attack": "Each time you take the Attack action during your turn, you can make two attacks rather than one.", "Ranger|Roving": "Your Speed goes up by 10 feet as long as you aren't wearing Heavy armor. You also gain a Climb Speed and a Swim Speed, both equal to your Speed.", "Ranger|Expertise": "Pick two skills you're proficient in but don't yet have Expertise in. You gain Expertise in both of them.", "Ranger|Tireless": "Primal forces now sustain you on your journeys. **Temporary Hit Points.** As a Magic action, you can roll 1d8 and add your Wisdom modifier, then gain that many Temporary Hit Points (minimum of 1). The number of times you can take this action equals your Wisdom modifier (minimum of once), and finishing a Long Rest restores all expended uses. **Decrease Exhaustion.** After each Short Rest you finish, your Exhaustion level drops by 1, if you have any Exhaustion.", "Ranger|Relentless Hunter": "When you take damage, it can't break your Concentration on Hunter's Mark.", "Ranger|Nature's Veil": "You invoke nature spirits to hide you with magic. As a Bonus Action, you can turn invisible, having the Invisible condition until your next turn ends. Your uses of this feature equal your Wisdom modifier (minimum of once), and finishing a Long Rest restores all expended uses.", "Ranger|Precise Hunter": "Your attack rolls have Advantage against whichever creature your Hunter's Mark currently marks.", "Ranger|Feral Senses": "Your bond with the forces of nature gives you Blindsight with a range of 30 feet.", "Ranger|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. The recommended choice is Boon of Dimensional Travel.", "Ranger|Foe Slayer": "Your Hunter's Mark now uses a d10 for its damage die instead of a d6.", "Rogue|Expertise": "Choose two skills you're proficient in. You gain Expertise in them. If you're proficient in Sleight of Hand and Stealth, those two are recommended. When you reach Rogue level 6, choose two more skills you're proficient in, and you gain Expertise in those as well.", "Rogue|Sneak Attack": "You know how to strike subtly and make the most of a foe's distraction. Once per turn, if you have Advantage on an attack roll that uses a Finesse or Ranged weapon and the attack hits a creature, you can deal an extra 1d6 damage to that creature. This extra damage is the same type as the weapon's damage. You don't need Advantage on the roll if an ally of yours that doesn't have the Incapacitated condition is within 5 feet of the target and you don't have Disadvantage on the roll. The extra damage grows as you gain Rogue levels, as the Sneak Attack column of the Rogue Features table shows: 1d6 at level 1, increasing by 1d6 at every odd Rogue level up to 10d6 at level 19.", "Rogue|Thieves' Cant": "Working your roguish trade in various communities taught you several languages. You know Thieves' Cant plus one other language of your choice from the Standard Languages and Rare Languages tables.", "Rogue|Weapon Mastery": "Choose two kinds of weapons you're proficient with, such as Daggers and Shortbows. Your weapon training lets you use the mastery properties of those weapons. After you finish a Long Rest, you can change which kinds of weapons you chose. For instance, you could trade them for Scimitars and Shortswords.", "Rogue|Cunning Action": "Your quick thinking and agility let you move and act fast. During your turn, you can take the Dash, Disengage, or Hide action as a Bonus Action.", "Rogue|Steady Aim": "As a Bonus Action, you grant yourself Advantage on your next attack roll this turn. This works only if you haven't moved yet this turn. Using it also drops your Speed to 0 for the rest of the current turn.", "Rogue|Cunning Strike": "You've found cunning new ways to use your Sneak Attack. Whenever your Sneak Attack deals damage, you can apply one of the Cunning Strike effects below. Every effect has a die cost: the number of Sneak Attack damage dice you give up to add it. You remove those dice before rolling, and the effect happens right after the attack deals its damage. For example, to add the Poison effect, you take 1d6 away from the Sneak Attack damage before you roll. When a Cunning Strike effect calls for a saving throw, the DC is 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus and Dexterity modifier. **Poison (Cost: 1d6).** You lace your strike with toxin, and the target makes a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, it has the Poisoned condition for 1 minute. The Poisoned target makes the save again at the end of each of its turns, and a success ends the effect on it. You can use this effect only while you carry a Poisoner's Kit. **Trip (Cost: 1d6).** A Large or smaller target makes a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, it has the Prone condition. **Withdraw (Cost: 1d6).** Right after the attack, you move a distance of up to half your Speed, and this movement doesn't provoke Opportunity Attacks.", "Rogue|Uncanny Dodge": "When an attack roll from an attacker you can see hits you, you can take a Reaction to cut the damage you take from that attack in half (round down).", "Rogue|Evasion": "You can nimbly sidestep certain dangers. If an effect gives you a Dexterity saving throw to take half damage, a successful save means you take no damage, and a failed save means you take only half damage. This feature doesn't work while you have the Incapacitated condition.", "Rogue|Reliable Talent": "If you make an ability check that draws on a skill or tool you're proficient with, any d20 roll of 9 or lower can count as a 10 for you.", "Rogue|Improved Cunning Strike": "Whenever you deal Sneak Attack damage, you can apply up to two Cunning Strike effects, paying each effect's die cost.", "Rogue|Devious Strikes": "You've practiced devious new ways to use your Sneak Attack. These effects join your Cunning Strike options. **Daze (Cost: 2d6).** The target makes a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, then on its next turn it can only move, take an action, or take a Bonus Action, choosing just one of those. **Knock Out (Cost: 6d6).** The target makes a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, it is knocked out, having the Unconscious condition until it takes any damage or 1 minute passes. The Unconscious target makes the save again at the end of each of its turns, and a success ends the effect on it. **Obscure (Cost: 3d6).** The target makes a Dexterity saving throw. If it fails, it has the Blinded condition until its next turn ends.", "Rogue|Slippery Mind": "Your cunning mind is extremely hard to control. You are proficient in Wisdom and Charisma saving throws.", "Rogue|Elusive": "You're so evasive that attackers seldom get the upper hand against you. Attack rolls against you can't have Advantage unless you have the Incapacitated condition.", "Rogue|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. The recommended choice is Boon of the Night Spirit.", "Rogue|Stroke of Luck": "You have an uncanny knack for succeeding when it matters. When you fail a D20 Test, you can change the roll to a 20. You can use this feature again only after you finish a Short or Long Rest.", "Sorcerer|Spellcasting": "Drawing on your innate magic, you can cast spells. The rules below show how you cast your Sorcerer spells, which come from the Sorcerer spell list. **Cantrips.** You know four Sorcerer cantrips of your choice. Recommended picks are Light, Prestidigitation, Shocking Grasp, and Sorcerous Burst. Each time you gain a Sorcerer level, you can swap one of your cantrips from this feature for a different Sorcerer cantrip of your choice. At Sorcerer levels 4 and 10, you learn one more Sorcerer cantrip of your choice, as the Cantrips column of the Sorcerer Features table shows. **Spell Slots.** The Sorcerer Features table lists how many spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all your expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** You prepare a list of the level 1+ spells you can cast with this feature. To begin, pick two level 1 Sorcerer spells. Recommended picks are Burning Hands and Detect Magic. The Prepared Spells column of the Sorcerer Features table shows how your list grows as you gain Sorcerer levels. Each time that number goes up, pick more Sorcerer spells until your list holds as many spells as the table shows. Each chosen spell's level must be one for which you have spell slots. For instance, a level 3 Sorcerer can have six Sorcerer spells of level 1 or 2, in any mix, on the prepared list. If another Sorcerer feature gives you spells that are always prepared, those spells don't count against the number you can prepare with this feature, but they still count as Sorcerer spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** Each time you gain a Sorcerer level, you can swap one spell on your list for a different Sorcerer spell, as long as you have spell slots of that spell's level. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your spellcasting ability for Sorcerer spells is Charisma. **Spellcasting Focus.** An Arcane Focus can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Sorcerer spells.", "Sorcerer|Innate Sorcery": "Something in your past left a permanent mark on you, filling you with simmering magic. You can release that magic as a Bonus Action, and it lasts 1 minute. During that time, the spell save DC of your Sorcerer spells goes up by 1, and you have Advantage on attack rolls for Sorcerer spells you cast. You have two uses of this feature, and finishing a Long Rest restores all expended uses.", "Sorcerer|Font of Magic": "You can draw on the wellspring of magic inside you. Sorcery Points represent this wellspring, and you spend them to create various magical effects. You start with 2 Sorcery Points and gain more at higher levels, as the Sorcery Points column of the Sorcerer Features table shows (the number equals your Sorcerer level). Your Sorcery Points can't exceed the number the table gives for your level. Finishing a Long Rest restores all expended Sorcery Points. Your Sorcery Points fuel the options below, as well as other features that use them, such as Metamagic. **Converting Spell Slots to Sorcery Points.** By expending a spell slot, you gain Sorcery Points equal to that slot's level. Doing so requires no action. **Creating Spell Slots.** You can use a Bonus Action to transform Sorcery Points you haven't spent into a single spell slot. Each slot level's point cost and the minimum Sorcerer level needed to create it appear in the Creating Spell Slots table: a level 1 slot costs 2 points (Sorcerer level 2), a level 2 slot costs 3 points (level 3), a level 3 slot costs 5 points (level 5), a level 4 slot costs 6 points (level 7), and a level 5 slot costs 7 points (level 9). The highest slot level you can create is 5. Slots you create this way disappear when you finish a Long Rest.", "Sorcerer|Metamagic": "Since your magic comes from within, you can reshape your spells to fit your needs. You gain two Metamagic options, chosen from the Metamagic Options list. You use them to modify spells temporarily as you cast them. Each option has a Sorcery Point cost, which you must spend to use it. When you cast a spell, you can apply only one Metamagic option to it, unless an option says otherwise. Each time you gain a Sorcerer level, you can swap one of your Metamagic options for one you don't know. At Sorcerer levels 10 and 17, you gain two more options each time.", "Sorcerer|Sorcerous Restoration": "Upon finishing a Short Rest, you can regain some expended Sorcery Points. The total can't exceed half your Sorcerer level (round down). After you use this feature, you need to finish a Long Rest before you can use it again.", "Sorcerer|Sorcery Incarnate": "If your Innate Sorcery has no uses remaining, you can still activate it by spending 2 Sorcery Points as you take the Bonus Action to do so. Also, whenever your Innate Sorcery is active, you can apply up to two of your Metamagic options to each spell you cast.", "Sorcerer|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. The recommended choice is Boon of Dimensional Travel.", "Sorcerer|Arcane Apotheosis": "As long as your Innate Sorcery is active, once on each of your turns you can use one Metamagic option without spending any Sorcery Points on it.", "Warlock|Eldritch Invocations": "You have uncovered Eldritch Invocations: fragments of forbidden knowledge that give you a lasting magical ability or teach you other lessons. Pick one invocation to start with, such as Pact of the Tome. The invocations are detailed in the Eldritch Invocation Options section of the Warlock class. **Prerequisites.** You must meet an invocation's prerequisite, if it has one, to learn it. For example, an invocation that requires a level 5+ Warlock becomes available to you when you reach Warlock level 5. **Replacing and Gaining Invocations.** Each time you gain a Warlock level, you can swap one of your invocations for another one you qualify for. You can't swap out an invocation that is a prerequisite for another invocation you have. The Eldritch Invocations column of the Warlock Features table shows when you gain more invocations of your choice. You have 3 at level 2, 5 at level 5, 6 at level 7, 7 at level 9, 8 at level 12, 9 at level 15, and 10 at level 18. Unless an invocation's description says otherwise, you can't pick it more than once.", "Warlock|Pact Magic": "An occult ceremony bound you in a pact to a mysterious entity, and that bond grants you magical powers. The entity speaks as a voice in the shadows and its identity stays unclear, yet the boon it gave you is concrete: you can cast spells. The rules below show how you cast your Warlock spells, which come from the Warlock spell list. **Cantrips.** You know two Warlock cantrips of your choice. Recommended picks are Eldritch Blast and Prestidigitation. Each time you gain a Warlock level, you can swap one of your cantrips from this feature for a different Warlock cantrip of your choice. At Warlock levels 4 and 10, you learn one more Warlock cantrip of your choice, as the Cantrips column of the Warlock Features table shows. **Spell Slots.** The Warlock Features table lists how many spell slots you have for casting your Warlock spells of levels 1 to 5, and the level of those slots. All of your slots share the same level. You have 1 slot at level 1, 2 at level 2, 3 at level 11, and 4 at level 17. The slot level is 1 at level 1 and rises to 2 at level 3, 3 at level 5, 4 at level 7, and 5 at level 9. Finishing a Short or Long Rest restores all expended Pact Magic spell slots. For example, a level 5 Warlock has two level 3 spell slots. If that Warlock casts Witch Bolt, a level 1 spell, it must use one of those slots, so the spell takes effect at level 3. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** You prepare a list of the level 1+ spells you can cast with this feature. To begin, pick two level 1 Warlock spells. Recommended picks are Charm Person and Hex. The Prepared Spells column of the Warlock Features table shows how your list grows as you gain Warlock levels. Each time that number goes up, pick more Warlock spells until your list holds as many spells as the table shows. A chosen spell's level can't be higher than the table's Slot Level column shows for your level. For instance, at level 6 you learn a new Warlock spell, and it can be of level 1, 2, or 3. If another Warlock feature gives you spells that are always prepared, those spells don't count against the number you can prepare with this feature, but they still count as Warlock spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** Each time you gain a Warlock level, you can swap one spell on your list for a different Warlock spell of an eligible level. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your spellcasting ability for Warlock spells is Charisma. **Spellcasting Focus.** An Arcane Focus can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Warlock spells.", "Warlock|Magical Cunning": "You can perform a 1-minute esoteric rite. When it ends, you regain expended Pact Magic spell slots, up to half your maximum number of slots (round up). After you use this feature, you need to finish a Long Rest before you can use it again.", "Warlock|Contact Patron": "You once reached your patron mostly through intermediaries, but now you can speak with it directly. The Contact Other Plane spell is always prepared for you. With this feature, you can cast that spell to contact your patron without expending a spell slot. When you do, you automatically succeed on the spell's saving throw. After you cast the spell this way, you need to finish a Long Rest before you can do so again.", "Warlock|Mystic Arcanum": "Your patron gives you a magical secret known as an arcanum. Pick one level 6 Warlock spell to be this arcanum. You can cast the arcanum spell one time without expending a spell slot, and then you need to finish a Long Rest before casting it this way again. You gain another Warlock spell of your choice that you can cast this way at Warlock level 13 (a level 7 spell), level 15 (a level 8 spell), and level 17 (a level 9 spell), as the Warlock Features table shows. Finishing a Long Rest restores all uses of your Mystic Arcanum. Each time you gain a Warlock level, you can swap one of your arcanum spells for a different Warlock spell of the same level.", "Warlock|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. The recommended choice is Boon of Fate.", "Warlock|Eldritch Master": "Your Magical Cunning feature now restores every expended Pact Magic spell slot when you use it.", "Wizard|Spellcasting": "Your study of arcane magic has taught you to cast spells. The rules below show how you cast your Wizard spells, which come from the Wizard spell list. **Cantrips.** You know three Wizard cantrips of your choice. Recommended picks are Light, Mage Hand, and Ray of Frost. Whenever you finish a Long Rest, you can swap one of your cantrips from this feature for a different Wizard cantrip of your choice. At Wizard levels 4 and 10, you learn one more Wizard cantrip of your choice, as the Cantrips column of the Wizard Features table shows. **Spellbook.** Your wizardly apprenticeship ended with the creation of a unique book: your spellbook. It is a Tiny object that weighs 3 pounds and has 100 pages, and only you or someone casting Identify can read it. You choose what the book looks like and what it's made of, for example a gilt-edged tome or a stack of vellum tied with twine. Your spellbook contains every level 1+ spell you know. At first, it has six level 1 Wizard spells of your choice. For these six, the suggested picks are Mage Armor, Magic Missile, Sleep, Thunderwave, Detect Magic, and Feather Fall. With every Wizard level you gain after level 1, you write two more Wizard spells of your choice into your spellbook. You must have spell slots of each added spell's level, as the Wizard Features table shows. These spells are the fruit of the arcane research you do regularly. **Spell Slots.** The Wizard Features table lists how many spell slots you have for casting your level 1+ spells. Finishing a Long Rest restores all your expended slots. **Prepared Spells of Level 1+.** You prepare a list of the level 1+ spells you can cast with this feature. To do this, pick four spells from your spellbook. Each chosen spell's level must be one for which you have spell slots. The Prepared Spells column of the Wizard Features table shows how your list grows as you gain Wizard levels. Each time that number goes up, pick more Wizard spells until your list holds as many spells as the table shows. Here too, each spell's level must be one for which you have spell slots. For instance, a level 3 Wizard can prepare six spells from the spellbook, of level 1 or 2 in any mix. If another Wizard feature gives you spells that are always prepared, those spells don't count against the number you can prepare with this feature, but they still count as Wizard spells for you in every other way. **Changing Your Prepared Spells.** After each Long Rest you finish, you can revise your prepared list, swapping any of its spells for other spells from your spellbook. **Spellcasting Ability.** Your spellcasting ability for Wizard spells is Intelligence. **Spellcasting Focus.** Either an Arcane Focus or your spellbook can serve as your Spellcasting Focus for Wizard spells. **Expanding and Replacing a Spellbook.** The spells you add as you gain levels reflect your ongoing magical research, but you might also find other spells on your adventures to add to the book. For example, you could find a Wizard spell on a Spell Scroll and then add it to your spellbook. **Copying a Spell into the Book.** When you come across a level 1+ Wizard spell, you can add it to your spellbook, provided it is of a level you can prepare and you have time to transcribe it. Each level of the spell costs 2 hours of transcription and 50 GP. Once that is done, you can prepare the spell just as you prepare the others in your spellbook. **Copying the Book.** You can transcribe a spell from your spellbook into a different book. The process works like adding a new spell to your spellbook, but it is quicker since you already know how to cast the spell: each level of the copied spell needs just 1 hour and 10 GP. Should your spellbook be lost, the same procedure lets you transcribe the Wizard spells you have prepared into a new spellbook. To complete the remainder of that new book, you need to find new spells. That risk is why many wizards maintain a spare spellbook.", "Wizard|Ritual Adept": "If a spell in your spellbook has the Ritual tag, you can cast it as a Ritual. The spell doesn't need to be prepared, but casting it this way requires you to read from the book.", "Wizard|Arcane Recovery": "Studying your spellbook lets you regain some of your magical energy. When you finish a Short Rest, you can pick expended spell slots to recover. Their combined level can't exceed half your Wizard level (round up), and no slot of level 6 or higher can be recovered this way. For example, a level 4 Wizard can recover up to two levels' worth of slots, regaining either one level 2 slot or two level 1 slots. After you use this feature, you need to finish a Long Rest before you can use it again.", "Wizard|Scholar": "Your magical studies also led you to specialize in a second field. From the following list, pick one skill you have proficiency in: Arcana, History, Investigation, Medicine, Nature, or Religion. You gain Expertise in that skill.", "Wizard|Memorize Spell": "After a Short Rest, you can look through your spellbook and swap one level 1+ Wizard spell that your Spellcasting feature has prepared for another level 1+ spell written in the book.", "Wizard|Spell Mastery": "Your command of certain spells is so complete that you can cast them at will. Pick one level 1 spell and one level 2 spell from your spellbook whose casting time is an action. Both are always prepared for you, and you can cast either one at its lowest level without using a spell slot. Casting either of them at a higher level requires a spell slot. Each time a Long Rest ends, you can review your spellbook and trade one of those spells for a different eligible spell of that same level found in the book.", "Wizard|Epic Boon": "You gain an Epic Boon feat, or another feat of your choice that you qualify for. Boon of Spell Recall is the recommended pick.", "Wizard|Signature Spells": "Pick two level 3 spells from your spellbook to be your signature spells. They are always prepared for you. Each of them can be cast once at level 3 without spending a spell slot. After casting one this way, you must finish a Short or Long Rest before you can cast that spell this way again. Casting either one at a higher level requires a spell slot."};
/* v19 RD17: the twelve "<Class> Subclass" features */
CF_FULL["Barbarian|Barbarian Subclass"]="At level 3 you pick a Barbarian subclass: Path of the Zealot, Path of the World Tree, Path of the Wild Heart, or Path of the Berserker. You choose it in the builder, on the Class step. A subclass is a path of specialization that hands you features at set Barbarian levels. From then on, you have every feature of your subclass whose level is equal to or lower than your Barbarian level.";
